<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hippias (tyrant)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 490 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Hippias of Athens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hippias was tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> from 527 to 510 BCE, the eldest son of Peisistratos. After his brother Hipparchus was murdered in 514 BCE his rule grew harsh, and he was expelled with Spartan help in 510. He married his daughter to Aeantides of Lampsacus, took refuge with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and accompanied their army to Marathon in 490 BCE, hoping to be restored.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hippias-tyrant.jpg" alt="Hippias (tyrant)" width="320" height="491" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Author David Eugene Smith Publisher Ginn and company · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Number_Stories_of_Long_Ago_-_Plate_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hippias was born around 570 BCE as the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Pisistratus</a>, the first tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. When his father was forced to flee to Eretria after offending Megakles, Pisistratus took counsel with his sons, and Hippias proposed that they retake the tyranny. Athens fell to Pisistratus for the third time in 546 BCE. Hippias was supported in the restoration by the family of the polemarch Charmus, an old ally of his father, and Pisistratus gave him Charmus's daughter in marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hippias succeeded his father as tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 528 or 527 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Pisistratus</a> died of old age. He was a patron of poets and craftsmen, and Athens enjoyed prosperity under his rule. His brother Hipparchus, who may have ruled jointly with him, was murdered at the Panathenaic festival in 514 BCE by Harmodius and Aristogeiton. Aristogeiton resented Hipparchus's advances toward Harmodius, and the plotters meant to kill both brothers.</p><p>Only Hipparchus died. Hippias's soldiers captured the group, and Aristogeiton was executed after torture while Harmodius was killed on the spot. It was said that Hippias afterwards became bitter and cruel over four years, unlike his moderate father. He executed many citizens, exiled others and imposed harsh taxes.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcmaeonidae">Alcmaeonidae</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Pisistratus</a> had exiled in 546 BCE, were concerned about his alliances with the Persian ruling class and planned an invasion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>, who had been archon in 525/4 BCE, is said to have bribed the Pythian priestess of Delphi to tell the Spartans to liberate the Athenians. After a failed expedition, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-i">Cleomenes I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> invaded in 510 BCE and trapped Hippias on the Acropolis, took the children of the Peisistratids hostage and made him leave to have them returned. Power passed to the Athenian magistrates, which began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athenian-democracy">Athenian democracy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hippias went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigeion">Sigeion</a>, then to Lampsakos and to King Darius in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. The Spartans later tried to restore him, but the Corinthians and other allies refused to see a tyranny imposed on a Greek city. He made his illegitimate son Hegesistratus tyrant of Sigeion and railed against the Athenians to Artaphrenes, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>. In 490 BCE he urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, accompanied the Persian fleet and proposed Marathon as suited to their cavalry. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> he dreamt the night before that he lay with his mother, which he took as an omen of a return, but a loose tooth fell out on the beach.</p><p>He is said to have died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> on the way back from Marathon. His five sons joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a>'s army in 480 BCE, and the Peisistratids never again had influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Building continued under his rule on the temple of Olympian Zeus, which was not finished until Hadrian's time.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hippias (tyrant)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 6th century BCE the tyrant Hippoclus and his son Aeantides ruled Lampsacus under Persia; Aeantides married a daughter of <b>Hippias of Athens</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lampsacus">Lampsacus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Marathon, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-500" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hippias (tyrant)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-500">What was here in 500 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
